mbent is retiring, and every expert rater plus prediction markets point to a Powell flip. Powell's cash and outside-spending advantage reinforce that, though the single poll showing a narrower race and Harding's solid fundraising keep this short of Safe.", "watch": "A strong Harding debate performance on Oct. 14 or a late shift in the affordability-focused campaign could tighten the race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Don Bacon (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Nebraska's 2nd District is an open seat this cycle with Republican incumbent Don Bacon retiring, and the district's slight Democratic lean plus a favorable national environment put the GOP on defense. Democrat Denise Powell has out-raised Republican Brinker Harding and benefits from roughly $3.1 million in outside support, while Harding has drawn little outside help despite raising over $2 million himself. National Republicans, including Steve Scalise, have campaigned for Harding in Omaha, signaling the party sees the seat as genuinely contested.\n\nEvery major expert rater calls the race a likely Democratic flip, with ratings ranging from Tilt D to Safe D, and prediction markets price Powell's chances near 90 percent. The lone recent poll shows Powell up four points, narrower than fundamentals suggest, so some caution is warranted. The race is best characterized as Likely Democratic, with affordability the dominant campaign theme and an October debate a potential inflection point.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9278, "margin": 8.8}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9035, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.914, "margin": 8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9146, "margin": 8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8905, "margin": 8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9055, "margin": 8.5}]}